title: '[[id:0d49b634-b190-48b0-8656-c99f824d19c0][Federated wiki followed by forking for a decade before skg: its users say what reporting deep changes, and marks on forks, should and should not be]]'
pid: f67d5dce-d3ba-41e4-a35c-1412f66f47cf
body: |2-
  Open it before designing what a follower is told has changed below
  the first layer, a mark on a note someone has forked, or a record in
  a fork of the original it copied. Read on 2026-11-15, it holds three
  lessons: whole copies drift, so deep reporting should be notices and
  not copies; a fork here keeps no time or state of its original, so
  it cannot say, as federated wiki's "twins" do (pages of the same
  title on other sites, flagged newer or older), that its original is
  newer; and marks the author can see make whether a follower
  takes an edit mean more, a cost to weigh in the design's question
  of where processed marks live.
